# Workflow Rules

## Worktree Discipline
- At startup, discover and remember the branch or worktree assigned to your role.
- If your assigned worktree is `master`, work in the main project checkout on
  its current branch. Do not expect or create a `.worktrees/<role>` directory
  for that role.
- Work only in your assigned branch or worktree.
- Do not inspect, diff, merge, or base work on another branch unless a handoff
  or an explicit operator instruction names that branch.
- Do not run `./swarm` from an agent worktree to repair helper scripts. If the
  handoff helpers are missing from PATH, stop and report the startup failure.

## Commit Messages
- Include your role byline in every commit message in this form: `By <role>.`
- Example:

```text
Add retry policy to PaymentGateway adapter

By coder.
```

- A commit-msg hook appends `By <role>.` when it is missing. Do not skip the
  hook with `--no-verify`.

## Temporary Files
- Use `./tmp/` in your assigned worktree for scratch files and handoff drafts.
- Do not use `/tmp`.
- Do not use `.swarmforge/handoffs/outbox/tmp/` as scratch; that belongs to the
  handoff helper.

## Asking The Operator
- When blocked by ambiguity, contradiction, or a conflict between the task and
  the existing code, ask with
  `pack_dashboard_request.sh clarify ./tmp/question.txt`.
- Do not ask in the pane. Do not send a `note` handoff to ask a question.

## Failure Conditions
- If the expected git layout or your assigned worktree is missing, stop and
  report instead of silently working in the wrong place.
- If the repository's build is already broken before you start, report that
  before making changes.
